I’m not saying you were foolish for believing it. You learned it from repeated experiences and trusted people around you, so it makes sense that it feels obvious and personal.

What I’m saying is that the belief may be blending together several real things:

- athletes being pushed to prioritize football over school;
- demanding schedules leaving less time for academics;
- some players struggling with grades or career transition;
- genuine risks from concussions and repeated head impacts;
- a sports culture that can reward toughness while discouraging asking for help.

Those can produce the impression that “football players are less sharp,” especially if that’s what you repeatedly saw. But they don’t mean an individual player has less intelligence or less potential because he plays football.

You don’t have to abandon concern for your cousin. You can turn it into something more useful: encourage him to protect his health, take any learning or memory issues seriously, seek tutoring or career guidance if needed, and build skills and interests outside the game.
